---
# CAN THE PROJECTION RATE STILL BE EXPLAINED? -- the question the read-model panels could not
# answer for a whole evening, because half the answer had no metric.
#
# `ReadModelProjector` is fed by THREE change-stream cursors, and for a long time only one of them
# was counted:
#
#   kinowo_worker_movie_change_events_total{country,op}        the `movies` cursor
#   kinowo_worker_screenings_change_events_total{country,op}   the `screenings` cursor -- one event
#       per changed screenings DOCUMENT, i.e. per (film, cinema slot), and normally the LARGER of
#       the three by an order of magnitude
#   kinowo_worker_movie_slots_change_events_total{country,op}  the `movie_slots` cursor -- one event
#       per changed slot DOCUMENT, added 2026-09-07 when 96 (film, venue) pairs whose slot landed
#       after the film's last projection turned out never to be projected again; metered from its
#       first day precisely because of what this rule says about a third trigger
#
# An event costs AT MOST one `kinowo_worker_readmodel_project_calls_total` — at most, because the
# side-collection apply COALESCES: an event naming a film an apply is already queued for rides that
# apply instead of buying its own (counted by `kinowo_worker_screenings_coalesced_changes_total` and
# `kinowo_worker_movie_slots_coalesced_changes_total`), since the apply re-reads the film's current
# state and one read after a burst sees all of it. So a healthy worker projects at or BELOW the sum
# of the three event rates, never above it. That asymmetry is why this rule only looks upward. On
# 2026-09-04 worker-de logged 184
# movies events against 10,114 projections in 85 minutes and every panel that could have named the
# cause plotted only the movies line -- which was flat. The number was not wrong, it was
# unattributable, and there is no threshold that catches "unattributable". A RATIO against the
# metered input is the only thing that does.
#
# WHAT THIS DELIBERATELY DOES NOT ALERT ON.
#
#   The redundant-write share. `kinowo_worker_screenings_writes_total{outcome="unchanged"}` counts
#   writes the store recognised as no-ops and dropped. A HIGH share there is the guard EARNING ITS
#   KEEP, not a fault -- `replaceFilm` is film-wide while its callers' change is one venue, so a
#   wide release legitimately offers hundreds of unchanged rows per scrape (297 of 298 on prod DE).
#   Alerting on it would page for healthy operation, which is how a channel gets ignored. It is a
#   panel, and the number to read once this rule has already told you something is wrong.
#
#   Projections with no read-model output. `readmodel_writes_total` sits near zero even when
#   projection is hot -- that is what the metadata-reuse cache is FOR -- so "hot with no output" is
#   the normal shape, not the broken one.
#
# THE `country` LABEL comes from the SCRAPE (scrape-kinowo-apps.yaml labels each worker's NodePort
# target), not from the application, so it is true by construction.
groups:
  - name: read-model-projection
    rules:
      # -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
      # THE PROJECTOR IS RUNNING FAR AHEAD OF ANYTHING THAT COULD BE TRIGGERING IT.
      #
      # 3x, not 1.1x. An event buys at most one projection, but the two rates are measured over
      # the same window from different counters and a burst that straddles the window edge skews
      # the ratio either way. Under 1 is ROUTINE and never interesting: coalescing pushes it down
      # by design, and a cursor that reopens and replays from its resume token counts its events
      # again. Three times the metered input is far outside any of that, and the shape this
      # exists for ran at FIFTY-FIVE times.
      #
      # THE 0.5/s FLOOR IS THE POINT OF THE RULE, not decoration. At PL's quiet-hour 0.03/s a
      # single stray projection against zero events is an infinite ratio, and every country idles
      # there overnight. The floor is what keeps this rule about volume that costs something: 0.5
      # projections/s is already above every country's measured baseline over 2026-09-01..04
      # (PL 0.1-0.6, DE 0.02-0.4, US 0.6-1.4).
      #
      # A MISSING COUNTER MAKES THIS SILENT, not loud: the sum of three instant vectors is empty
      # when any one has no series, so this rule says nothing until a build that exports all of
      # them is deployed. That is the safe direction, and `ReadModelProjectionMetricsAbsent` below
      # is what notices the metrics have gone entirely.
      - alert: ReadModelProjectionTriggerUnaccounted
        expr: >-
          (
            sum by (country) (rate(kinowo_worker_readmodel_project_calls_total[30m]))
            > 3 * (
              sum by (country) (rate(kinowo_worker_movie_change_events_total[30m]))
              + sum by (country) (rate(kinowo_worker_screenings_change_events_total[30m]))
              + sum by (country) (rate(kinowo_worker_movie_slots_change_events_total[30m]))
            )
          ) > 0.5
        # ONE HOUR. The rate windows are already 30m, so this is not waiting for the condition to
        # be real -- it is waiting out a worker rollout, where a restarted cursor replays from its
        # resume token and both counters restart at zero at slightly different moments. The fleet
        # redeployed five times in the four hours before this rule was written.
        for: 1h
        labels:
          severity: warning
        annotations:
          summary: "{{ $labels.country }} is projecting {{ $value | printf \"%.2f\" }}/s more than its change streams can explain"
          description: >-
            `readmodel_project_calls_total` is running more than 3x the combined rate of the three
            cursors that are supposed to drive it, so something is projecting rows that no metered
            change-stream event asked for. Panel 47 on the application dashboard plots all four
            series together and panel 48 draws the sum. Check, in order: whether a FOURTH trigger
            has been added and not metered (that is what this rule exists to catch -- the
            `screenings` cursor itself was unmetered until 2026-09-05 and cost an evening);
            whether `ReadModelProjector.reconcile` is being called by something other than a
            backfill script (the scheduled sweep re-projects nothing and logs "reprojected 0
            doc(s)", so its log line settles that in one grep); and whether a cursor is reopening
            in a loop, which replays its resume token and inflates projections without inflating
            the event counters proportionally.

      # -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
      # THE READ MODEL IS DROPPING FILMS FASTER THAN RE-KEYING EXPLAINS.
      #
      # `reconcile` removes a film's derived documents when its source row vanishes or its
      # filmId changes, and the film's whole schedule goes with it -- the card 404s until the
      # new key propagates. Most prunes are RE-KEYS and completely routine (see
      # `movies deletes are mostly re-keys`), so the signal cannot be "a prune happened". It is
      # a prune BURST measured against the corpus it is eating.
      #
      # A FRACTION, not a rate. Absolute rates are not comparable across countries whose
      # corpora differ 12x (ES 192 films, US 2,302), and a threshold tuned on US volume would be
      # most of the ES catalogue. Dividing by `corpus_movies{subset="total"}` makes one number
      # mean the same thing everywhere: the share of a country's films that left the read model
      # in an hour.
      #
      # `max by (country)` OVER THE CORPUS, `sum by (country)` OVER THE PRUNES — the asymmetry is
      # deliberate and the two halves are different KINDS of number. Exactly one worker publishes a
      # country's corpus census, so two series for it are the SAME census counted twice, while the
      # prune counter is the opposite: each process counts its own removals, so when two are alive
      # their totals genuinely add and `max` there would drop half the prunes.
      #
      # WHAT THAT DUPLICATE ACTUALLY COST, MEASURED, so the next reader does not over-read this.
      # On 2026-09-06 18:26-18:30Z the `instance` relabel (21b381727) had Prometheus answering for
      # every worker under both its old and new name until the old one fell out of lookback, and
      # `sum by (country)` doubled the census for all five: PL 943 -> 1,886, UK 1,576 -> 3,152,
      # US 2,267 -> 4,534, DE 1,848 -> 3,696, ES 191 -> 382. FOUR MINUTES. That is shorter than
      # this rule's `for: 15m` and much shorter than its sibling's `for: 2h`, so THAT event could
      # not have flipped either alert's state and did not — the only rule it actually paged was
      # kinowo-showtime-volume-collapsed, whose `max_over_time` baseline latched the artefact for
      # six hours. Here the bite is smaller and worth naming exactly: while it lasts, the ratio
      # this rule tests is halved (which can reset a pending streak and delay a real page by up to
      # `for:`), the `{{ $value }}` it reports is half the truth, and the sibling's summary names
      # DOUBLE the invisible films — sending an operator after a row that does not exist. A
      # duplicate that outlives the `for:` window, which a scrape config listing one worker twice
      # would give you, turns all of that into an outright miss.
      #
      # NOT A ROLLOUT, whatever `TtlIndexUnreconciled` below used to say: the worker Deployment is
      # `replicas: 1` with `strategy: Recreate`, so two worker targets are never live at once.
      #
      # 30%, from the measured week. Over the 7 days to 2026-09-05 the worst HEALTHY hour any
      # country reached was PL at 16.9% (DE 9.4%, ES 7.8%, US 4.6%, UK 3.7%), with p99s of
      # 1.0-5.4% -- so 30% sits at ~1.8x the worst real sample and far above every typical one.
      # The shape it exists for is much larger than that: the 2026-08-10 BackfillReadModel wipe
      # took Poznan from 209 films to 28 in one pass.
      #
      # WHAT THIS DOES NOT CATCH, deliberately: ONE film disappearing. A single row is ~0.05% of
      # a corpus and no aggregate threshold can separate it from routine re-keying -- on
      # 2026-09-05 clearing one film's resolution by hand pruned `lalka|2026` with 148 screenings
      # and 404'd its page, and nothing here would have moved. That case is caught by reading the
      # removal audit (`kinowo.removal-audit` logs every card with its screening count), not by a
      # rule. This is the MASS-loss alert.
      #
      # NO absent() COMPANION for this one, unlike its siblings below: a labelled counter has no
      # series until its first observation, so a country that has pruned nothing is legitimately
      # absent and an absence rule would page for a read model doing no harm. The projector's own
      # liveness is already covered by ReadModelProjectionMetricsAbsent.
      # THE DENOMINATOR HAS TO BE A REAL CORPUS, which is why the trailing `> 0` is not
      # decoration. `WorkerCorpusMetrics` SEEDS every subset at 0 on boot and its
      # `publish` writes nothing on a scan that could not read the whole corpus, so a
      # restart into a failing read leaves `corpus_movies{subset="total"}` at a genuine
      # 0 for as long as the read stays broken — the 2026-07-27 decode bug held exactly
      # that for ~50 minutes. The numerator is unaffected, since the pre-restart samples
      # are still inside the `[1h]` window. A `clamp_min(..., 1)` denominator turned
      # that into "pl dropped 4200% of its films" — a critical page for a census
      # outage, which is the failure this rule's own preamble rejects the
      # showtime-volume rules for. `kinowo-corpus-census-stuck` owns that shape.
      # ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
      # THE SILENT HALF OF THE MIS-RESOLUTION SWEEP. `CinemaCorroboration` finds a row whose own
      # cinemas contradict the film it resolved to, and `UnresolvedTmdbReaper` re-resolves it.
      # Rejecting the wrong film and finding the right one are SEPARATE steps, and the second can
      # fail alone: the row is then left unresolved, stops satisfying `readyToProject`, and the
      # projector prunes its card -- so the film is invisible on the site while its venues still
      # sell tickets for it. For a row carrying screenings that is worse than the wrong poster it
      # started with, and nothing else reports it: the reaper logs a re-try but never an outcome,
      # every other census gauge gates on `readyToProject` and so drops these rows silently, and
      # ONE film is far below the ReadModelFilmPruneBurst ratio above.
      #
      # Threshold is a plain > 0 because the measured baseline IS zero: on 2026-09-06 all five
      # countries had 0 such rows (504 unresolved rows across the corpus, every one of them
      # concluded `tmdbNoMatch` with its cinema detail done, so all of them project and are
      # visible). Newcomers do not trip this either -- an unresolved new film incubates in
      # `pending_movies` and reaches `movies` already concluded. `for: 2h` rides out a genuine
      # transient (a re-key or title-rule wave leaving a row briefly un-concluded) without
      # letting a stuck row hide: the reaper's own retry period is 24h, so anything still here
      # after two hours is waiting a day, not a tick.
      - alert: ReadModelFilmsInvisibleWithScreenings
        expr: >-
          max by (country) (kinowo_worker_corpus_movies{subset="unresolved_with_showtimes"}) > 0
        for: 2h
        labels:
          severity: warning
        annotations:
          summary: "{{ $labels.country }} has {{ $value }} film(s) screening but invisible on the site"
          description: >-
            `kinowo_worker_corpus_movies{subset="unresolved_with_showtimes"}` counts rows that fail
            `readyToProject` while their cinemas still list an UPCOMING showtime, so the read model
            prunes their cards and the films 404 while tickets are on sale. Read the worker's
            `kinowo.removal-audit` log to name them (`reason=reconcile-prune`), then for each row
            compare the CINEMA slots' director and runtime against the film it resolved to and
            search TMDB for the title the venues actually publish. Write the correct `tmdbId` and
            TMDB slot directly -- do NOT clear `tmdbId` to force a re-resolve, because that prunes
            the card within minutes and `UnresolvedTmdbReaper` will not revisit the key for up to
            24h. Its sibling `subset="misresolved"` counts the sweep's INPUT: that falling while
            this rises is the sweep trading wrong films for invisible ones. Full method in
            `docs/misresolution-sweep.md`.

      - alert: ReadModelFilmPruneBurst
        expr: >-
          (
            (
              sum by (country) (increase(kinowo_worker_readmodel_films_pruned_total[1h]))
              / max by (country) (kinowo_worker_corpus_movies{subset="total"})
            ) > 0.3
          )
          and on (country) (max by (country) (kinowo_worker_corpus_movies{subset="total"}) > 0)
        # The increase window is already an hour, so this is not waiting for the condition to be
        # real -- it is confirming the ratio is not a single scrape-boundary sample.
        for: 15m
        labels:
          severity: critical
        annotations:
          summary: "{{ $labels.country }} dropped {{ $value | humanizePercentage }} of its films from the read model in an hour"
          description: >-
            `kinowo_worker_readmodel_films_pruned_total` has removed more than 30% of
            `corpus_movies{subset="total"}` for this country within an hour, so schedules are
            disappearing from the site with the films that carried them. The worst healthy hour
            measured over the week to 2026-09-05 was 16.9%. Read the worker's `kinowo.removal-audit`
            log first -- it names every card removed with its screening count and a reason
            (reconcile-prune / scrape-prune) -- then check whether a backfill or reprojection
            script is running against this country, whether a city slug was renamed (which
            re-keys every CityScreening and orphans the old ones), and whether the source
            `movies` rows still exist: a prune whose rows are intact is a projection fault, and
            a prune that follows rows actually vanishing is a scrape or cleanup fault.

      # -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
      # THE SITE IS SERVING FEWER FILMS THAN THE CORPUS SAYS IT SHOULD.
      #
      # Every rule above watches an EVENT -- a prune rate, a projection rate, a row that will not
      # resolve. This one watches the RESULT, and 2026-09-07 is why it has to exist separately.
      # A boot reconcile removed ~500 PL cards at 04:06Z; ReadModelFilmPruneBurst caught the prune
      # and cleared 25 minutes later, because the prunes had stopped. THE HOLE HAD NOT CLOSED. It
      # was still 84 cards wide when that alert resolved, 71 four hours later, and nothing anywhere
      # was watching it -- the film-count rules compare a city against its own recent past, which
      # by then included the loss. An alert on the cause cannot tell you the effect is over.
      #
      # THE TWO GAUGES ARE BUILT TO BE SUBTRACTED, and that is not an assumption -- it is what
      # `kinowo_worker_movies_served`'s own HELP text says it is for ("the projection-side mirror
      # of the web's kinowo_web_movies_served, for spotting read-model drift"). Verified on prod
      # rather than taken on trust: identical `isUpcoming` predicate in each city's own zone,
      # identical scope split, identical city roster wired from the same source, and an identical
      # published city count on both sides (us 468, de 158, uk 79, es 52, pl 41 -- no unmatched
      # series in either direction). The worker counts what the corpus WOULD serve; the web counts
      # what it IS serving. The difference is cards that should exist and do not.
      #
      # 3%, AND THE BACKTEST THAT NEARLY GOT THIS WRONG. Over the 7 days to 2026-09-06 this ratio
      # sat at a US median of 4.7% and a p99 of 16.0%, against PL's 0.16% -- overlapping PL's
      # 15.25% incident outright, which says no single threshold can work and the rule needs a
      # per-country table with a ~25% US carve-out. That reading was wrong, and the shape of the
      # mistake is worth keeping: THE US BASELINE WAS ITSELF THE DEFECT. The ratio decayed
      # monotonically all week (us 18.9% -> 3.3%, uk 4.4% -> 1.8%, de 3.5% -> 0.4%) and stepped to
      # a floor for every country at once when the stable-film-id re-key landed around
      # 2026-09-07T00:00Z: de 0.02%, es 0.00%, pl 0.22%, uk 0.21%, us 0.08%. Those ~1,000 US cards
      # were real ones the web was not serving -- the WEB side rose 720 to meet the worker, the
      # worker did not fall to meet the web. Calibrating on the week would have sized this rule
      # around a bug and permanently excused the largest country. 3% is ~14x the post-fix floor and
      # well under half the incident it exists for.
      #
      # THE MECHANISM, since knowing it is what says the old baseline cannot come back. Per
      # 95737a5f1's own message the card id used to be re-derived as `sanitize(title)|resolvedYear`,
      # so two DISTINCT source rows could compute the SAME card id. The worker gauge counts one
      # film per source row; the web gauge joins its screenings to cards BY THAT ID. A collision
      # therefore cost the web a card permanently while the worker went on counting both rows --
      # a standing, invisible gap rather than a lag. The id is now the row's FilmId (a variant is
      # the film id plus its variant title), and two rows cannot collide on that, so the CLASS is
      # gone and not merely its instances.
      #
      # DO NOT PREDICT THIS RATIO FROM CORPUS SIZE, tempting as the US number makes it. Measured at
      # one instant, 2026-09-06T12:00Z: DE held the second-LARGEST corpus at 1,835 rows and the
      # second-smallest gap at 0.32%, while the UK on 249 FEWER rows (1,586) ran 5.7x that at
      # 1.83%. The pair inverts at 2026-08-31 too, so it is not an artefact of a single sample.
      #
      # WHY DE AND UK INVERT IS NOT ESTABLISHED, and this comment deliberately stops short of
      # saying. The obvious guess is that collisions track title-and-year DENSITY rather than row
      # count -- an English title space full of re-releases and encore screenings colliding more
      # than a German-localised one -- but that is an inference from one inverted pair and it is
      # NOT measured. It was put to the corpus and came back inconclusive: no country has a single
      # colliding `key` group left to count, because the write path now MERGES a row whose key
      # another row already holds (a8d381113), so the fix erases the evidence its own diagnosis
      # would need. Grouping on sanitised title alone, ignoring year, found reuse only in DE and PL
      # and none in UK or US -- which if anything leans against the density guess. Settling it
      # needs a pre-migration snapshot of `movies`, which does not exist.
      #
      # What survives is the instruction, which does not depend on the cause: a country whose gap
      # looks wrong for its size is not a measurement error to be explained away, and corpus size
      # is not a proxy for expected drift.
      #
      # THE CALIBRATION IS THIN AND SHOULD BE REDONE. It rests on roughly six hours of post-fix
      # baseline, taken while PL was still mid-incident. Re-run the backtest once PL has closed and
      # tighten or loosen from a full week. The direction of the error matters more than its size:
      # too tight pages during a projection change of ours, too loose is another four hours nobody
      # hears about.
      #
      # `for: 45m` because the author of the projection path puts the legitimate disagreement at
      # one prune tick (30 min) even across a change of theirs -- the boot heal runs before the
      # first prune -- so anything still here at 45 minutes is a hole, not a lag.
      #
      # THE `and on (country)` GUARD IS DEFENSIVE SYMMETRY, NOT LOAD-BEARING -- said plainly here
      # because the obvious reading of it is wrong. A zero census does NOT page this rule even
      # without the guard: this numerator is corpus MINUS served, so a worker side that reads 0
      # while the web still serves 435 gives -435, and no negative clears a `> 0.03` threshold.
      # Measured, not reasoned: the same shape through `clamp_min(..., 1)` evaluates to -435 too.
      # That is the opposite of ReadModelFilmPruneBurst above, whose numerator is a prune COUNT
      # and stays positive, so a clamped denominator there really did report "pl dropped 4200% of
      # its films". The guard is kept for the 0/0 NaN and to keep the two rules reading alike; it
      # is not what makes a census outage safe here. The DIRECTION is.
      - alert: ReadModelServingShortOfCorpus
        expr: >-
          (
            (
              (
                sum by (country) (max by (country, city) (kinowo_worker_movies_served{scope="all"}))
                - sum by (country) (max by (country, city) (kinowo_web_movies_served{scope="all"}))
              )
              / sum by (country) (max by (country, city) (kinowo_worker_movies_served{scope="all"}))
            ) > 0.03
          )
          and on (country) (
            sum by (country) (max by (country, city) (kinowo_worker_movies_served{scope="all"})) > 0
          )
        for: 45m
        labels:
          severity: critical
        annotations:
          summary: "{{ $labels.country }} is serving {{ $value | humanizePercentage }} fewer films than its corpus holds"
          description: >-
            `kinowo_worker_movies_served{scope="all"}` is the films this country's corpus WOULD
            serve and `kinowo_web_movies_served{scope="all"}` is what the site IS serving; they
            have disagreed by more than 3% for 45 minutes, so cards that should be on the site are
            missing and their schedules with them. Unlike ReadModelFilmPruneBurst this watches the
            HOLE rather than the event that made it, so it stays lit until the read model is whole
            again -- on 2026-09-07 the burst alert cleared with 84 cards still gone. Find WHICH
            cards per city with
            `max by (city) (kinowo_worker_movies_served{country="{{ $labels.country }}",scope="all"})
            - max by (city) (kinowo_web_movies_served{country="{{ $labels.country }}",scope="all"})`,
            then read the worker's `kinowo.removal-audit` log for what removed them. The known
            shape is a reconcile prune whose cards the boot heal then declines to re-project: the
            2026-09-07 one deleted VARIANT cards (`<filmId>~<variant>`, the decorated listings like
            "Poranek dla dzieci: Pucio") while each film's plain card survived, and a heal that
            only projected rows with NO card skipped every one of them. If this is firing during a
            deliberate projection change, it is telling you the change has not finished landing.

      # -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
      # THE COMPANION, in the shape mongodb.rules and cinema-scrape.rules use throughout: a rule
      # that can only fire on a series has to be paired with one that fires on the series'
      # ABSENCE, or the monitoring's own failure looks exactly like health. The rule above is
      # silent when its inputs are missing, and silence is also what a well-behaved projector
      # looks like.
      #
      # `absent()` OVER THE WHOLE METRIC, not per country: a per-country form would page for four
      # countries throughout any deliberate single-country stop (the 2026-08-02 poland-only month).
      - alert: ReadModelProjectionMetricsAbsent
        expr: absent(kinowo_worker_readmodel_project_calls_total)
        # ONE HOUR: longer than a rollout of every worker in the fleet.
        for: 1h
        labels:
          severity: warning
        annotations:
          summary: "No worker is publishing read-model projection metrics"
          description: >-
            `kinowo_worker_readmodel_project_calls_total` has no series from any country, so
            ReadModelProjectionTriggerUnaccounted cannot fire and the read model's whole input
            path is unwatched. Either every worker is down -- in which case TargetDown in
            monitoring-self.rules is also firing and is the better alert to read -- or the
            projector is no longer wired to `WorkerTaskMetrics`, which TargetDown would NOT catch
            because the pod is up and serving /metrics without this family in it.

      # -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
      # ITS OWN COMPANION, and it needs a separate one: ReadModelServingShortOfCorpus is a
      # SUBTRACTION, and a binary operator drops every pair it cannot match. Lose either gauge and
      # the rule does not report an infinite shortfall -- it reports nothing at all, which is the
      # exact failure it was written to end. Losing the WEB side is the dangerous direction: the
      # site serving nothing would look identical to the site serving everything.
      #
      # `absent()` over each metric separately so the summary names WHICH side went, and over the
      # whole metric rather than per country, matching ReadModelProjectionMetricsAbsent above.
      - alert: ReadModelServedGaugesAbsent
        expr: >-
          absent(kinowo_worker_movies_served) or absent(kinowo_web_movies_served)
        # ONE HOUR, matching its siblings: longer than a full fleet rollout.
        for: 1h
        labels:
          severity: warning
        annotations:
          summary: "One side of the read-model drift comparison has stopped publishing"
          description: >-
            Either `kinowo_worker_movies_served` or `kinowo_web_movies_served` has no series from
            any country, so ReadModelServingShortOfCorpus cannot fire -- its subtraction drops
            unmatched pairs and goes SILENT rather than loud. Whichever side is missing, the site
            is unwatched for cards that should be there and are not. If the WEB gauge is the one
            gone, check TargetDown in monitoring-self.rules first: a web tier that is down is the
            better alert to read. If the gauge is missing while the pod serves /metrics happily,
            the collector has been unwired from the tier's metrics registry, which TargetDown
            cannot see.

      # -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
      # TTL INDEXES THAT DO NOT MATCH THE CODE. Separate from the projection rules above and in this
      # file because it is the same shape: an app-published `kinowo_worker_*` gauge about whether
      # the pipeline's own storage is configured the way the code says.
      # -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
      - alert: TtlIndexUnreconciled
        # ZERO IS THE ONLY HEALTHY VALUE, so this is `> 0` and not a tuned threshold. The gauge is
        # always present and reads 0 when every TTL index agrees with its constant, which is why it
        # can be compared rather than tested for presence.
        # PER COUNTRY, because `max()` alone drops every label and an alert that cannot name the
        # pod cannot be acted on -- the collection is in that worker's log, and you have to know
        # whose log to read. `max by (country)` also survives one worker being SCRAPED TWICE --
        # under an old and a new `instance` name across a relabel, say -- without double-firing.
        # (This used to say "two workers during a rollout", which cannot happen: the Deployment is
        # `replicas: 1` with `strategy: Recreate`.)
        expr: max by (country) (kinowo_worker_ttl_index_mismatches) > 0
        # TEN MINUTES: the reconciler runs at construction, so a rollout is the only time this can
        # legitimately flicker while pods come up on a new constant and rebuild their indexes.
        for: 10m
        labels:
          severity: warning
        annotations:
          summary: "{{ $labels.country }}: {{ $value }} TTL index(es) do not match what the code asks for"
          description: >-
            A TTL index's `expireAfterSeconds` disagrees with the constant in the code AND the app
            could not rebuild it. THE COSTLY CASE IS AN INDEX THAT IS NOW ABSENT: the reconciler
            drops and recreates (`collMod` is not in `readWrite`), so a drop that succeeded and a
            create that failed leaves the collection reaping NOTHING and growing without bound --
            check `detailCache-*` and `uptimeBuckets` sizes first. WHICH collection is in the
            worker log, not in this metric: `kubectl -n kinowo logs deploy/worker-{{ $labels.country }} | grep -i
            "TTL index"` names it, with both expiries and the database it is in. This class of drift was invisible until
            2026-09-06, when `detailCache-cinema-city` was found still reaping at 6h a day after
            the code moved it to 2h, so treat a sustained reading here as real.

      # THE COMPANION, in the shape this file and mongodb.rules use throughout: a comparison cannot
      # fire when its series is missing, and a metric that has gone away looks exactly like health.
      - alert: TtlIndexMetricAbsent
        expr: absent(kinowo_worker_ttl_index_mismatches)
        # ONE HOUR, matching ReadModelProjectionMetricsAbsent: longer than a full fleet rollout.
        for: 1h
        labels:
          severity: warning
        annotations:
          summary: "No worker is publishing the TTL-index reconciliation gauge"
          description: >-
            `kinowo_worker_ttl_index_mismatches` has no series, so `TtlIndexUnreconciled` cannot
            fire and nothing is watching whether the TTL constants in the code are the ones Mongo
            is enforcing. Either every worker is down -- TargetDown in monitoring-self.rules is the
            better alert then -- or `TtlIndexMetrics.register` has been dropped from
            `WorkerMetrics`, which TargetDown would NOT catch because the pod serves /metrics
            perfectly well without this family in it.
